182
00:13:28,943 --> 00:13:32,918
Q108: "Does Git know which branch
a branch was created from?

183
00:13:32,958 --> 00:13:38,339
What is the closest it can offer, and why
is a pull request's base branch not the

184
00:13:38,379 --> 00:13:39,142
same thing?"

185
00:13:41,482 --> 00:13:46,391
Answer out loud. A strong answer goes through
the places where such a fact could be

186
00:13:46,431 --> 00:13:51,280
stored, and says what each holds. It names
the local trace and the heuristic, with

187
00:13:51,320 --> 00:13:55,990
the limits of both. Then it separates the
layers: what Git stores, and what the

188
00:13:56,030 --> 00:13:56,944
platform stores.

189
00:13:58,784 --> 00:14:04,101
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: two branches have diverged when

190
00:14:04,141 --> 00:14:09,254
neither tip is an ancestor of the other.
The merge base is their best common

191
00:14:09,294 --> 00:14:14,543
ancestor, and I count each side from it
with git rev-list --left-right --count

192
00:14:14,583 --> 00:14:19,561
A...B. git merge-base --is-ancestor
answers ancestry with its exit status.

193
00:14:19,901 --> 00:14:23,609
In git log, two dots and three
dots select sets of commits.

194
00:14:23,649 --> 00:14:26,912
In git diff, three dots
means "from the merge base".

195
00:14:26,952 --> 00:14:31,294
Git records no parent branch, so I
name the base in every comparison.

196
00:14:31,334 --> 00:14:36,375
And ahead and behind against origin/main
